# Container & deploy layout

Two services. `qbr` is `nginx:alpine` serving the static deck on `:8080`;
`sf-sync` is a Flask/gunicorn sidecar on `:8081`.

## Both Dockerfiles COPY files explicitly

Neither image copies a directory wholesale. A new page, script, or Python
module that is not added to the relevant `COPY` line **will not exist in the
container**, and the failure looks like a stale-cache bug rather than a
missing file. When you add a file, add the `COPY`:

- new `*.html` / `*.js` → root `Dockerfile`
- new `sf-sync/*.py` → `sf-sync/Dockerfile`

## The shared accounts volume

`./accounts` on the host is bind-mounted into both containers — read-only at
`/usr/share/nginx/html/accounts` for `qbr`, read-write at `/data/accounts` for
`sf-sync`. This is what makes a Salesforce pull show up in the SLA report with
no copy step.

Do not convert this to a named volume. Do not bake `accounts/` into an image.
It is gitignored and holds real customer data.

## Healthchecks

Use `127.0.0.1`, never `localhost` — on Alpine `localhost` can resolve
IPv6-only and busybox `wget` then fails against an IPv4 listener, marking a
perfectly healthy container unhealthy. Keep an explicit `--start-period` so
startup isn't counted as failure.

## No egress after build

The root image's only network step is the font-fetch build stage. The runtime
container makes zero outbound requests, which is what lets this handle real
customer data. Don't introduce a runtime fetch, a CDN reference, or a
telemetry call.

## Compose conventions

- `restart: unless-stopped` on both services.
- `.env` is `required: false` — the deck must come up without Salesforce
  credentials. Only `sf-sync` degrades.
- `.env` is never committed; changes go to `.env.example` with placeholders.

## Keep versions aligned

`SYNC_VERSION` appears in `sf-sync/Dockerfile`, `.env.example`, and
`k8s/env-configmap.yaml`. Bump them together, and mirror any compose change
into the matching `k8s/*.yaml` manifest.
